{# gfwfbn/templates/wfbn/emails/confirm.html -- the "click to confirm your
   email address" mail, sent by routes/wfbn/updates.ts on subscribe.

   IT EXTENDS THE SHELL, as Django's does. It previously did not: updates.ts
   built three bare <p> strings inline, on the reasoning that the chrome was
   "not built yet" -- but emails/page.njk has been ported since, and a
   multipart mail whose HTML part is indistinguishable from its text part
   looks, in an inbox, exactly like a mail that sent no HTML at all. Which
   is how this was reported.

   Django hardcodes https://www.givefood.org.uk into every link here; the
   port passes `confirm_url` and `foodbank_url` in already built from
   SITE_DOMAIN, so the same absolute URLs come out without the literal
   domain being restated in a template. #}
{% extends "emails/page.njk" %}

{% block body %}

    <p>Please click the button below to confirm your email address and get updates from {{ foodbank_name }} food bank.</p>

    {# Django's btn/btn-primary table wrapper, kept verbatim: nested
       presentation tables are how a button survives Outlook, and page.njk
       carries the matching styles. #}
    <table role="presentation" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" class="btn btn-primary">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
            <td align="left">
                <table role="presentation" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0">
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                    <td> <a href="{{ confirm_url }}" target="_blank">Confirm my email address</a> </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
                </table>
            </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p></p>
    <p></p>
    <p></p>
    <p></p>
    <p>If you're not expecting this email then please ignore it.</p>

{% endblock %}
